sec.p:
The height at every point in the image is determined differently in 2D and 3D QPI. In 3D QPI, the height h(x, y) is determined by a form of thresholding of the 3D refractive index n(x, y, z) in the z-axis [22]. In 2D QPI, the height is the integral of Δn along the optical axis and inversely proportional to the mean dry mass concentration:
(3)
h(x,y)=1αc¯d∫Δn(x,y,z)dz. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} h(x, y)=\frac{1}{\alpha \bar{c}_{d}} \int \Delta n(x, y, z) d z . \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Segmentation of images is used to identify the area Ai occupied by each cell i in the monolayer. The 2.5D-approximation used for both 2D and 3D QPI assumes that the cell i fills the volume under the area Ai defined by the segmentation, and thus the cell volume is defined as Vi = Aihi, where hi is the average height of the cell i. The general formula (2) for the dry mass of the cell i is, in the 2.5D-approximation evaluated as follows:
(4)
2D QPI:md,i=c¯dAihi=c¯d∬Aih(x,y)dxdy=1α∬Ai(∫−∞∞Δn(x,y,z)dz)dxdy \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \quad m_{d, i}=\bar{c}_{d} A_{i} h_{i}=\bar{c}_{d} \iint_{A_{i}} h(x, y) d x d y=\frac{1}{\alpha} \iint_{A_{i}}\left(\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} \Delta n(x, y, z) d z\right) d x d y$$\end{document}
(5)
3D QPI:md,i=1α∬Ai(∫0h(x,y)Δn(x,y,z)dz)dxdy. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \quad m_{d, i}=\frac{1}{\alpha} \iint_{A_{i}}\left(\int_{0}^{h(x, y)} \Delta n(x, y, z) d z\right) d x d y.$$\end{document}

We quantify the spatial and temporal height variations in the monolayer as the relative standard deviations:
(6)
σ^xy(ρ)=⟨h2⟩xy−⟨h⟩xy2⟨h⟩xy, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\sigma}_{x y}(\rho)=\frac{\sqrt{\left\langle h^{2}\right\rangle_{x y}}-\langle h\rangle_{x y}^{2}}{\langle h\rangle_{x y}}, \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(7)
σ^t(ρ¯)=⟨⟨h2⟩t−⟨h⟩t2⟨h⟩t⟩xy \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \hat{\sigma}_{t}(\bar{\rho})=\left\langle\frac{\sqrt{\left\langle h^{2}\right\rangle_{t}-\langle h\rangle_{t}^{2}}}{\langle h\rangle_{t}}\right\rangle_{x y}$$\end{document}
where h = h(x, y, ρ(t)). The temporal average is taken over 4 h, which has been found to be the timescale of oscillations in the projected area of MDCK [8]. ρ\begin{document}$\rho$\end{document} denotes the mean cell density in this time interval.

The volume and the height of a frustum are given by
(A1)
Vfrustum=hAa3(1+a+a),a=Ab/Aa, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{\text {frustum }}=\frac{h A_{a}}{3}(1+\sqrt{a}+a), \quad a=A_{b} / A_{a}, \tag{A1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Aa and Ab are the apical and basal surface areas, respectively. The height of the frustum is then
(A2)
h=3Vfrustum(1+a+a)Aa. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} h=\frac{3 V_{\text {frustum }}}{(1+\sqrt{a}+a) A_{a}} . \tag{A2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We can then express the projected volume as a function of the frustum volume
(A3)
Vprojected(t)=3Vfrustum(t)1+a(t)+a(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{\text {projected }}(t)=\frac{3 V_{\text {frustum }}(t)}{1+\sqrt{a(t)}+a(t)} . \tag{A3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
For a constant true volume, we see that the projected volume of a given cell may appear to fluctuate if a(t) is not constant.

Likewise, the volume of a prismatoid is
(A4)
Vprismatoid=hAa6(1+a+4b),a=Ab/Aa,b=Am/Aa, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{\text {prismatoid }}=\frac{h A_{a}}{6}(1+a+4 b), \quad a=A_{b} / A_{a}, \quad b=A_{m} / A_{a}, \tag{A4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where Am is the cross-sectional area at the midplane. And we obtain the projected volume and the height as functions of the prismatoid volume
(A5)
h=6Vprismatoid((1+a+4b)Aa, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} h=\frac{6 V_{\text {prismatoid }}}{\left((1+a+4 b) A_{a}\right.}, \tag{A5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(A6)
Vprojected(t)=6Vprismatoid(t)1+a(t)+4b(t). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} V_{\text {projected }}(t)=\frac{6 V_{\text {prismatoid }}(t)}{1+a(t)+4 b(t)} . \tag{A6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Similar to the case of frustums, we see that the prismatoid cells may appear to have volume fluctuations even if the true volume is constant, if a(t) or b(t) is not constant.

Given the analysis above, we consider two candidate correction models:
(B6)
Model A:ntrue=anTomocube+noffset \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text{Model A:} n_{\text {true }}=a n_{\text {Tomocube }}+n_{\text {offset }}$$\end{document}
(B7)
Model B:ntrue=nTomocube+noffset \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \text{Model B:} n_{\text {true }}=n_{\text {Tomocube }}+n_{\text {offset }}$$\end{document}
Model B is the special case a = 1 of Model A.

Under the Rytov approximation, the reconstructed RI is (Eq. (B4))
(B8)
nTomocube(r)=n0assumed+λ028π2n0assumedVrealrecon(r), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} n_{\text {Tomocube }}(\mathbf{r})=n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}+\frac{\lambda_{0}^{2}}{8 \pi^{2} n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}} V_{\text {real }}^{\text {recon }}(\mathbf{r}), \tag{B8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where n0assumed\begin{document}$n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}$\end{document} is the value of n0 used by the software (fixed at instrument configuration), and V recon is the reconstructed scattering potential. For the true RI we have
(B9)
ntrue(r)=n0true+λ028π2n0trueVrealtrue(r). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} n_{\text {true }}(\mathbf{r})=n_{0}^{\text {true }}+\frac{\lambda_{0}^{2}}{8 \pi^{2} n_{0}^{\text {true }}} V_{\text {real }}^{\text {true }}(\mathbf{r}) . \tag{B9} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
If we assume that the reconstruction faithfully recovers the spatial structure of Vreal (i.e. V recon ≈ V true), then the difference between the two expressions is
(B10)
ntrue−nTomocube=(n0true−n0assumed)+Vrealtrueλ028π2(1n0true−1n0assumed)≈δn0+Vrealtrue⋅λ028π2−δn0(n0true)2, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} n_{\text {true }}-n_{\text {Tomocube }} & =\left(n_{0}^{\text {true }}-n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}\right)+V_{\text {real }}^{\text {true }} \frac{\lambda_{0}^{2}}{8 \pi^{2}}\left(\frac{1}{n_{0}^{\text {true }}}-\frac{1}{n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}}\right) \\ & \approx \delta n_{0}+V_{\text {real }}^{\text {true }} \cdot \frac{\lambda_{0}^{2}}{8 \pi^{2}} \frac{-\delta n_{0}}{\left(n_{0}^{\text {true }}\right)^{2}}, \tag{B10} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where δn0=n0true−n0assumed\begin{document}$\delta n_{0}=n_{0}^{\text {true }}-n_{0}^{\text {assumed }}$\end{document}. For biological cells, the RI contrast is small: ntrue − n0 ≲ 0.05, so Vreal ≈ (2π/λ0)2 · 2n0 · (n − n0) is much smaller than n02\begin{document}$n_{0}^{2}$\end{document}, and the second term in Eq. (B10) is of order (n − n0) δn0/n0 ≪ δn0. In the biologically relevant parameter range, the scaling correction is therefore negligible, and the error reduces to a pure constant offset:
(B11)
ntrue(r)≈nTomocube(r)+noffset,noffset≈δn0+λ028π2n0ΔVDC. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} n_{\text {true }}(\mathbf{r}) \approx n_{\text {Tomocube }}(\mathbf{r})+n_{\text {offset }}, \quad n_{\text {offset }} \approx \delta n_{0}+\frac{\lambda_{0}^{2}}{8 \pi^{2} n_{0}} \Delta V_{\mathrm{DC}} . \tag{B11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

app.p:
The calibration is performed in four steps. We first shift h2D,g0(ρ)\begin{document}$h_{2 D, g}^{0}(\rho)$\end{document} so that it matches h2D,f0(ρ)\begin{document}$h_{2 D, f}^{0}(\rho)$\end{document}. To do this, we fit a second-order polynomial to h2D,f0(ρ)\begin{document}$h_{2 D, f}^{0}(\rho)$\end{document} and determine the additive offset that minimizes the deviation between this polynomial and h2D,g0(ρ)\begin{document}$h_{2 D, g}^{0}(\rho)$\end{document}. This correction is applied separately for each dish, so that all datasets from a given dish receive the same shift. Next, we shift Δn¯3D(ρ)\begin{document}$\Delta \bar{n}_{3 D}(\rho)$\end{document} of one 3D dataset so that its trend coincides with that of the other 3D dataset. We then model both the RI contrast and the 3D height as second-order polynomials in density: Δn¯(ρ;a)=a0+a1ρ+a2ρ2,h3D(ρ;θ)=θ0+θ1ρ+θ2ρ2\begin{document}$\Delta \bar{n}(\rho ; \mathbf{a})=a_{0}+a_{1} \rho+a_{2} \rho^{2}, \quad h_{3 D}(\rho ; \boldsymbol{\theta})=\theta_{0}+\theta_{1} \rho+\theta_{2} \rho^{2}$\end{document}. Finally, we correct the constant term a0\begin{document}$a_{0}$\end{document} by an additional offset a′\begin{document}$a^{\prime}$\end{document} and use the resulting RI model to rescale h2D0(ρ)\begin{document}$h_{2 D}^{0}(\rho)$\end{document} into agreement with h3D(ρ). The key quantity is the path integral of the true RI contrast, averaged over the monolayer, Δn¯true(ρ)=n(ρ)−n0\begin{document}$\Delta \bar{n}_{\text {true }}(\rho)=n(\rho)-n_{0}$\end{document}, which can be expressed in terms of both 2D and 3D measurements:
(C1)
2D QPI:∫dzΔn¯true(ρ)=0.04⋅h¯2D0(ρ), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { 2D QPI: } \quad \int d z \Delta \bar{n}_{\text {true }}(\rho)=0.04 \cdot \bar{h}_{2 D}^{0}(\rho), \tag{C1} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(C2)
3D QPI:∫dzΔn¯true(ρ)=(Δn¯3D(ρ)+a0′)h¯3D(ρ). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \text { 3D QPI: } \quad \int d z \Delta \bar{n}_{\text {true }}(\rho)=\left(\Delta \bar{n}_{3 D}(\rho)+a_{0}^{\prime}\right) \bar{h}_{3 D}(\rho) \text {. } \tag{C2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Figure 10B shows h2D(ρ) and h3D(ρ) after calibration, demonstrating that the 2D QPI heights are brought into agreement with the 3D QPI reference. The resulting calibrated mean RI contrast, Δn¯true\begin{document}$\Delta \bar{n}_{\text {true }}$\end{document}, as a function of cell number density is shown in Fig. 10C, and the corresponding dry mass concentration, c¯d=Δn¯true/α\begin{document}$\bar{c}_{d}=\Delta \bar{n}_{\text {true }} / \alpha$\end{document}, is shown in Fig. 10D.
